WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.393
One block, one colour.

2
00:00:01.553 --> 00:00:03.782
A printing block prints one colour.

3
00:00:03.942 --> 00:00:08.633
For two colours you cut two blocks: one for the light parts, one for the dark.

4
00:00:08.793 --> 00:00:13.437
The first colour is printed, left to dry, and then the second goes on top.

5
00:00:14.137 --> 00:00:15.344
Register marks.

6
00:00:15.504 --> 00:00:21.588
Two pencil crosses on the paper, and two on the block, tell the second block exactly where to sit.

7
00:00:21.748 --> 00:00:26.299
Without them the colours land apart, however carefully you line them up by eye.

8
00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:28.903
Printed by hand in Rajasthan.

9
00:00:29.063 --> 00:00:36.911
In north-west India, printers press blocks of carved wood onto cloth by hand, one press at a time, one colour at a time.

10
00:00:37.071 --> 00:00:42.087
A whole cloth is hundreds of presses, and the rows step across so the joins do not show.

11
00:00:42.787 --> 00:00:44.366
One cloth, everybody's tiles.

12
00:00:44.526 --> 00:00:45.873
Your tile is yours.

13
00:00:46.033 --> 00:00:50.444
Sewn beside everyone else's, it becomes one cloth that nobody made alone.

14
00:00:50.604 --> 00:00:55.434
That is a collaborative work, and it only works if every tile is printed to last.
